 ## Overview
-Line Illustration Detection is a family of Non-Photorealistic Rendering (NPR) techniques designed to detect and render lines within volume data to communicate structure, shape, and boundaries [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visualizing complex geometry and internal structures in volumetric datasets where traditional photorealistic rendering may obscure topological details [S1, unofficial]. What sets this family apart from its siblings is its specific focus on line-based abstraction rather than surface shading or contour outlines; it distinguishes itself from [Face-based volume rendering with edge highlights](2453-face-based-volume-rendering-with-edge-highlights.md) by generating lines that represent intrinsic surface properties (like curvature) rather than just silhouette edges, and from [Outline and Contour Generation](4137-outline-and-contour-generation.md) by often utilizing vector-oriented or texture-based approaches that are viewpoint-independent or analytically derived, rather than purely image-space depth-difference extraction.
+Line Illustration Detection is a family of Non-Photorealistic Rendering (NPR) techniques designed to detect and render lines within volume data to communicate structure, shape, and boundaries [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visualizing complex geometry and internal structures in volumetric datasets where traditional photorealistic rendering may obscure topological details [S1, unofficial]. What sets this family apart from its siblings is its specific focus on line-based abstraction rather than surface shading or contour outlines; it distinguishes itself from [Face-based volume rendering with edge highlights](2453-face-based-volume-rendering-with-edge-highlights.md) by generating lines that represent intrinsic surface properties (like curvature) rather than just silhouette edges, and from [Outline and Contour Generation](4137-outline-and-contour-generation.md) by often utilizing vector-oriented or texture-based approaches that are viewpoint-independent or analytically derived, rather than purely image-space depth-difference extraction [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
-The family is unified by the categorization of detection methods into two primary paradigms: analytical and pixel-based [S1, unofficial].
-
-1.  **Analytical Approaches**: These are vector-oriented and resolution-independent [S1, unofficial]. They rely on explicit analytical representations of the model or volume data. Because they are vector-based, they can be transformed (rotated, scaled) without introducing aliasing artifacts [S1, unofficial]. They are considered more general and flexible but require the availability of explicit analytical forms [S1, unofficial].
-2.  **Pixel-Based Approaches**: These are image-space methods, often utilizing G-buffers (storing geometric properties like depth, normal, or color) [S1, unofficial]. They are generally faster and simpler to implement than analytical methods [S1, unofficial]. They are employed when explicit analytical representations are not available or practical [S1, unofficial]. A key advantage is that G-buffers can be reused without recalculation, and 2D image processing operators are fast [S1, unofficial].
-
-Both approaches aim to generate lines that enhance the perception of form, with the choice between them dictated by the availability of analytical data and performance constraints [S1, unofficial].
